afternoon  results from the nevada primary are  providing now a clearer picture of what  november and the election will look like  mail-in ballots though are accepted  through friday so some of those aren't  the very final vote totals quite yet but  two men very familiar with each other  they're going to go head-to-head for the  governor's seat governor steve sisilak  easily won the democratic nomination  over former clark county commissioner  tom collins and metro sheriff joe  lombardo has won the gop nomination with  38 percent of the vote overtaking his  competition reno attorney joey gilbert  and former senator dean heller with  sheriff lombardo running for governor  clark county is in need of a new sheriff  three police veterans were vying for the  top cop spot former undersheriff kevin  mcmahon won outright by a landslide with  more than half the vote 60 percent in  fact he served with metro for decades  and for the senate former attorney  general adam blacksad will be facing off  with democratic incumbent catherine  cortes masto he took more than half of  the vote despite a pretty strong  challenge for newcomer army veteran sam  brown  and several spots open for the clark  county school board of trustees these  are nonpartisan races which means that  if no one gets the 50 percent the top  two vote getters will face each other on  the ballot in november and it is a tight  race let's start with district d  stephen conger now has a slim lead over  irene cepeda who's the incumbent as for  district f irene bustamante a former  nevada assembly woman is ahead of  incumbent danielle four 21 to 18 percent  they'll likely be facing each other in  november as well and finally for  district g  linda cavazos  the only one of the incumbents in the  lead in her race and that'll also go to  a runoff against greg wieman  let's check the weather

